<article class="enc-article">
<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ali al-Rida</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 766–<a href="/years/818">818</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Ali ibn Musa al-Rida, Abū al-Ḥasan al-Thānī, al-Ridha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ali al-Rida was the eighth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a> of Twelver Shia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, a descendant of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a> born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, who succeeded his father Musa al-Kazim in 799. Departing from the anti-Shia policy of his predecessors, the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a> invited him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merv">Merv</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> and in 817 named him heir apparent, changing the official colour from Abbasid black to green. The appointment provoked an Abbasid revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, and as the court travelled back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> al-Rida died at Tus in 818, probably poisoned; many historians hold al-Ma'mun responsible. The city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mashhad">Mashhad</a> grew up around his grave, the holiest site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, visited by millions of Shia pilgrims every year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ali-al-rida.jpg" alt="Ali al-Rida" width="330" height="365" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sun'i · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chester_Beatty_T_414_fol_129r_Ali_al-Rida.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ali ibn Musa was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>. Sources give several years, from 148 AH (765/766 CE) to 153 AH, and the date often given by Shia authorities is 11 Dhu al-Qa'da 148 AH. His father, Musa al-Kazim, was the seventh Twelver Shia Imam, a descendant of Ali and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatima">Fatima</a>, and his mother was a freed slave, probably of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> origin, whose name appears as Najma or Tuktam. He lived with his father in Medina until 795/796 CE, and when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a> arrested Musa and took him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, he managed the family's property and the affairs of the Shias as his attorney.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Musa al-Kazim died in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> prison in 799/800, and Ali became his heir and successor under his father's will. He spent the next ten years in the reign of Harun. Like the fourth to seventh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imams</a>, he kept aloof from politics and refused to approve the Alid uprisings, which led the Zaydis, who favoured armed struggle, to oppose him. Yet he called the rule of his time a false government and held that obeying oppressors was wrong. He issued legal rulings in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophets-mosque">Prophet's Mosque</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> while in his twenties.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">Al-Ma'mun</a>, in a sudden departure from the anti-Shia policy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>, possibly meant to ease Shia revolts, invited him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merv">Merv</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> and named him heir apparent, though he accepted only on condition that he not interfere in government. Many Abbasids opposed this and installed Ibrahim ibn al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a> as anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. Al-Ma'mun married his daughter Umm Habib to al-Rida, and the marriage produced no children. He also arranged debates between scholars of different sects and religions in which al-Rida took part.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a>'s party left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, al-Rida fell ill and died at Tus around September 818, probably poisoned according to Shia sources; other dates range from September 817 to May 819. His death followed the assassination of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> al-Fadl ibn Sahl. Madelung, Hugh Kennedy and others consider al-Ma'mun responsible, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> historians al-Tabari and al-Masudi do not consider murder, and al-Masudi says he died from eating too many grapes.</p><p>Tus was later replaced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mashhad">Mashhad</a>, which grew around his grave. Works are attributed to him, among them the Al-Risalah al-Dhahabiah, a medical treatise, and the Sahifah of al-Ridha, 240 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadiths</a>; the authenticity of the treatise and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-jurisprudence">Fiqh</a> al-Rida is doubted. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> tradition he stands in the chain of authority through Ma'ruf al-Karkhi, though one scholar regards the accounts of their meeting as apocryphal. His son Muhammad al-Jawad continued the line of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imams</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ali al-Rida” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Alids were honoured and feared: al-Mansur crushed the revolt of the Alid al-Nafs al-Zakiyya in 762, while al-Ma’mun named the Alid <b>Ali al-Rida</b> his heir in 817 before abandoning the plan.</blockquote><a href="/societies/abbasid-caliphate">Social Hierarchy of the Abbasid Caliphate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>. Died in Tus, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=24.47,39.61&amp;year=799" aria-label="Medina on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5603 1263 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ali al-Rida"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6053 1533h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6053 1533h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=24.47,39.61&amp;year=799">What was here in 799 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
